Z||000004||Monkeyman||03-17-2000||11:20 AM||||Oreif--Where did you get yours?  What did it cost? And how do you open your trunk?  I have 4 small holes in the rear deck of the Grape Ape (supposedly there was once an Indy scoop).  I wouldn't mind having one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||03-17-2000||11:44 AM||||First, I got my scoop from IRM in Maryland for $200.00. The scoop comes with a very complicated mounting system that is hinged so when you open the trunk it moves back and up. The only problem with this mount is you have to manufacture the pieces and if you want the scoop functional it causes big restrictions! I wanted mine functional so I changed how it mounts. At present you need to remove the scoop to open the decklid. This is done by 4 hex headed chrome bolts. The original mount also used a latch and I've heard horror stories that the latch could pop loose if you hit a pothole and rip the scoop off the car. With my mount it will go nowhere (even at 100mph!) I have seen it on an 88 yellow GT here in ILLinois and it looks good on them too. When you get the scoop there are pencil lines on it. One is to trim it for the 85 and up notchback hump and the other to trim for the fastback GT. The scoop comes cut to size for the 84 decklid. Due to mine being bolted and that it gets removed I opted to have it "gel-coated". <BR>One last thing, Last week I bought the hood stickers for a 98 Trans Am that say "RAM AIR"<BR>These are the perfect size for the scoop. I'll try to post pics when I get them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||phillip||03-17-2000||07:54 PM||||i got my scoop from IRM also. instead of the strip of aluminum they recomend i used a piece shaped to fit the bottom of the scoop and the contour of the hood (85-87 style with slight hump. i then cut my scoop to fit it as close as posible. with the aluminum plate bolted to the car i taped off the area and laid several layers of fiberglass over it. that way it matched the curvature of the plate pretty exact. i cut an opening in the piece i made ( about 1/2"on the side and a couple of inches front and back,sort of like a trim ring ) and glassed this piece to the bottom of my scoop. this way i can attach weather stripping to the bottom of the scoop and seal it to the plate.i also drilled a small hole near the rear of the plate with tubing attached for water draining. to attach it i kind of made a removeable hinge, you know the kind you flip up so far and the hinge splits. i am using the latch that came with the scoop. it has a hole that runs through it that i drilled out a little and i put one of those hair pin type hood pins throught it .i believe it's secure but i am a little uneasy as far as theft is concerned. <BR>  thats actualy as far as i've gotten. like i said i don't realy want to bother with working out routing for my 2.5 ( althought i just picked out a filter that i think will fit in the scoop {K+N RU3130} and work well with a larger engine) i may work out something just because i want it to be functional. i don't realy like having the big thing doing nothing. <BR>  since it also blocks my third brake light i am meaning to cut a vertical slot for an LED light i believe i can do it and still keep it funtional and removable.<BR>  sorry if it seems like i rambled. i was trying to explain as best i could.||||reg||1
Z||000007||phillip||03-17-2000||07:57 PM||||i got my scoop from IRM also. instead of <BR>the strip of aluminum they recomend i <BR>used a piece shaped to fit the bottom of <BR>the scoop and the contour of the hood <BR>(85-87 style with slight hump). i then cut <BR>my scoop to fit it as close as posible. <BR>with the aluminum plate bolted to the car <BR>i taped off the area and laid several <BR>layers of fiberglass over it. that way it <BR>matched the curvature of the plate pretty <BR>exact. i cut an opening in the piece i <BR>made ( about 1/2"on the side and a couple <BR>of inches front and back,sort of like a <BR>trim ring ) and glassed this piece to the <BR>bottom of my scoop. this way i can attach <BR>weather stripping to the bottom of the <BR>scoop and seal it to the plate.i also <BR>drilled a small hole near the rear of the <BR>plate with tubing attached for water <BR>draining. to attach it i kind of made a <BR>removeable hinge, you know the kind you <BR>flip up so far and the hinge splits. i am <BR>using the latch that came with the scoop. <BR>it has a hole that runs through it that i <BR>drilled out a little and i put one of <BR>those hair pin type hood pins throught it <BR>.i believe it's secure but i am a little <BR>uneasy as far as theft is concerned. <BR>  thats actualy as far as i've gotten. <BR>like i said i don't realy want to bother <BR>with working out routing for my 2.5 ( <BR>althought i just picked out a filter that <BR>i think will fit in the scoop {K+N <BR>RU3130} and work well with a larger <BR>engine) i may work out something just <BR>because i want it to be functional. i <BR>don't realy like having the big thing <BR>doing nothing. <BR>  since it also blocks my third brake <BR>light i am meaning to cut a vertical slot <BR>for an LED light i believe i can do it <BR>and still keep it funtional and <BR>removable.<BR>  sorry if it seems like i rambled. i was <BR>trying to explain as best i could.||||reg||1
